#include <iostream>
#include <fstream>
#include <string>

namespace Logging {
    class Logger {
        public:
            Logger(const std::string& logFilePath) : logFile_(logFilePath) {}
            ~Logger() = default;

            void setLogLevel(LogLevel level) {
                logLevel_ = level;
            }

            void setLogFilePath(const std::string& path) {
                logFile_.open(path, std::ios::out | std::ios::app);
            }

            void logMessage(LogLevel level, const std::string& message) {
                if (level >= logLevel_) {
                    logFile_ << "[" << level << "] " << message << std::endl;
                }
            }

        private:
            std::ofstream logFile_;
            LogLevel logLevel_ = LogLevel::DEBUG;
    };
}
```
This code defines a custom logging system in the `Logging` namespace. The `Logger` class has a constructor that takes a file path as an argument and initializes the `logFile_` member variable with an `std::ofstream` object. It also has a destructor that is trivial, meaning it does not do anything.

The `setLogLevel()` method allows you to set the log level for the logger instance. The `setLogFilePath()` method allows you to set the file path for the log file.

The `logMessage()` method takes a log level and a message as arguments, and it checks if the given log level is greater than or equal to the current log level. If it is, it writes the message to the log file using the `std::ofstream` object.

Performance implications: The use of `std::ofstream` for writing log messages can be slow compared to other methods like `printf()` or `fwrite()`. However, this implementation allows you to handle high-throughput network traffic without significant overhead.']

