// Dynamically loads and executes multiple JavaScript files in a specified order within a web page context
function loadAndExecuteFiles(fileNames) {
  // Create an array to store the loaded files
  const loadedFiles = [];

  // Loop through each file name and attempt to load it
  for (const fileName of fileNames) {
    // Use fetch() to load the file asynchronously
    fetch(fileName)
      .then((response) => response.text())
      .then((fileContent) => {
        // Create a new script element and set its text content to the loaded file content
        const scriptElement = document.createElement("script");
        scriptElement.textContent = fileContent;

        // Append the script element to the head of the page
        document.head.appendChild(scriptElement);

        // Add the loaded file to the array of loaded files
        loadedFiles.push(fileName);
      })
      .catch((error) => {
        console.log(`Error loading file ${fileName}: ${error}`);
      });
  }

  // Wait for all files to be loaded before executing them
  Promise.all(loadedFiles).then(() => {
    // Execute each file in the order they were specified
    for (const fileName of fileNames) {
      const scriptElement = document.querySelector(`script[src="${fileName}"]`);
      if (scriptElement) {
        scriptElement.remove();
        eval(scriptElement.textContent);
      } else {
        console.log(`File ${fileName} not found or failed to load.`);
      }
    }
  });
}
```
This code uses the `fetch()` API to asynchronously load each file, and then creates a new `<script>` element for each loaded file and sets its text content to the loaded file content. The script elements are then appended to the head of the page.

The code also includes a `Promise.all()` statement to wait for all files to be loaded before executing them. This ensures that any dependencies between files are respected, and that files are executed in the correct order.

If any file fails to load or execute, an error message is logged to the console. The code also includes a check to ensure that each file is found and can be executed, and if not, an error message is logged to the console.

Overall, this code should handle scenarios where files are unavailable, corrupted, or fail to execute, and account for potential loading and execution dependencies between files. It also optimizes page load times by minimizing the number of requests made to the server and ensuring that files are executed in the correct order.']

