Here is an example of a C++ class that manages a dynamic list of custom `SensorData` objects, each containing sensor readings (temperature, humidity, and pressure) along with a timestamp. The class supports adding new data points, retrieving data by timestamp, and clearing all records. Additionally, it implements a feature to filter and return data points that fall within a specified temperature range.
```cpp
class SensorDataManager {
public:
    // Constructor
    SensorDataManager() : data_(), mutex_() {}

    // Add new data point
    void add(const SensorData& data) {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        data_.push_back(data);
    }

    // Retrieve data by timestamp
    std::vector<SensorData> getByTimestamp(uint64_t timestamp) const {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        std::vector<SensorData> result;
        for (const auto& data : data_) {
            if (data.timestamp == timestamp) {
                result.push_back(data);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

    // Clear all records
    void clear() {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        data_.clear();
    }

    // Filter and return data points that fall within a specified temperature range
    std::vector<SensorData> filterByTemperatureRange(double min, double max) const {
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(mutex_);
        std::vector<SensorData> result;
        for (const auto& data : data_) {
            if (data.temperature >= min && data.temperature <= max) {
                result.push_back(data);
            }
        }
        return result;
    }

private:
    // Data storage
    std::vector<SensorData> data_;

    // Mutex for thread safety
    mutable std::mutex mutex_;
};
```
This class uses a `std::vector` to store the sensor data, and a `std::mutex` to ensure thread safety when multiple threads are accessing and modifying the data simultaneously. The `add`, `getByTimestamp`, and `filterByTemperatureRange` methods are all thread-safe, while the `clear` method is not (it would need to be synchronized using the same mutex as the other methods).

The class also uses a fixed-size buffer for storing the sensor data, which can help reduce memory usage and improve performance. However, this approach may not be suitable for all use cases, particularly if the number of data points is expected to be large. In such cases, it may be more appropriate to use dynamic memory allocation based on usage patterns.

The class also includes a feature to filter and return data points that fall within a specified temperature range. This can be useful for applications where only certain temperature ranges are of interest. However, this feature should be used with caution, as it may result in unnecessary processing and memory usage if not properly implemented.']

